{
  "configuration_id": "config_028",
  "reason": "Evidence: All four completed experiments use scvi_n200 as the RNA encoder. The best configuration (config_006: scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l + transformer + weighted) achieves 0.7250 accuracy at only 3.60 GPU hours, and the image encoder (ViT-L >> ResNet50), aggregation (transformer > avgpool), and fusion (weighted ≥ concat) are now well-characterized. The main untested variable that could push accuracy higher is the RNA encoder itself. Question: Does replacing scvi_n200 with scgpt (a deep generative model for single-cell RNA) while keeping all other components at their best-performing settings (imagenet_vit_l, transformer, weighted) yield a meaningful accuracy gain? This is the cleanest single-variable ablation on the most impactful remaining design choice. Why this experiment: With 5 experiments left, testing the RNA encoder under the already-validated best pipeline is the highest-information experiment. If scgpt improves accuracy, it opens a new exploitation direction; if not, it confirms scvi_n200 is sufficient and we can focus remaining budget on profile setting or fine-tuning. Expected cost: ~4–5 GPU hours (scgpt is comparable in training cost to scvi_n200, and the ViT-L + transformer pipeline was ~3.6–4.7 h in prior runs), so it is moderate and well within budget."
}

